# Builtin library — visible from every module, lowest precedence:
# a custom module's own definitions and environment win.
#
# The environment a running module is handed (viba-interpreter.md). Read as
# a type, `Env` is an Environment — that is what a module's `$env` parameter
# asks for; read as computation it is the real environment, and a module reads
# it as `args.env` (with `args = __get_args__ << __decl__`). `Environment` is
# a plain function of the environment and the sub-environment's name, which is
# how a module call hands a module its own — `$viba_path` is where its imports
# are looked up, and a child keeps the parent's — and `$tmp_env` is the same
# thing under a name nobody chose, fresh every call. Both take the environment
# as their first argument, so nothing is bound to the environment they came
# from: `args.env.sub_env << args.env << "child"` is the same call as
# `$sub_env << args.env << "child"` (viba-interpreter.md).
#
# The members below walk the chain a child belongs to: `$get_root` is the
# environment at the top of it (`nil` in, `nil` out), `$get_relative_path` is an
# address seen from a root (`"b"` for `root/a/b` seen from `root/a`), and `$next_sibling` makes a
# new directory beside one — `a/b/c` answers `a/b/c0`, `a/b/c0` answers
# `a/b/c1` — refusing a name already taken. `$find_by_relative_path` is the way
# back: a relative path and the root to read it from, and with no root it
# searches from the environment it was read off, which is what
# `args.env.find_by_relative_path << "a/b"` means.
Environment =
    Object
  * $viba_path str
  * $sub_env (Environment <- $env Env <- $sub_env_name str)
  * $tmp_env (Environment <- $env Env)
  * $get_root ((Environment | nil) <- $current (Environment | nil))
  * $get_relative_path (str <- $current Environment <- $root (Environment | nil))
  * $find_by_relative_path (Environment <- $relative_path str <- $root (Environment | nil))
  * $next_sibling (Environment <- $current Environment)

Env = Environment

# Hands back what it receives unchanged: `echo << $x V` answers V given an environment. The
# work handed out is computed in the environment and at the time its receiver decides
# (branch.py); one name on the left, so this writes `builtin` and its member `$echo`.
builtin =
    $echo
    (
        $x Any
      <- $env Env
      <- $x Any
      <- { hand the argument back }
    )
